# Blybot configuration template (spec section 12).
#
# On Toolforge, copy to a 0600-permission file in the tool home directory
# and source it from run.sh. NEVER commit a filled-in copy.

# --- required -------------------------------------------------------------
# Set a bot token for EVERY platform you want this instance to run. On
# Toolforge, deploy-instance.sh starts one isolated job per platform whose
# token is present here (blybot-telegram, blybot-discord, ...) — you do not
# hand-create per-platform instances. Both platforms share this file and the
# ToolsDB (rows are platform-tagged), so one config drives them all.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Discord — see the setup runbook in docs/OPERATIONS.md (create the app/bot,
# enable Message Content + Server Members intents, invite with
# bot + applications.commands).
#DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
# IRC — no account or token to create: set the server to enable the job. The
# bot has no durable DMs there, so digest subscriptions stay off (by
# capability, automatically); capture and the wiki sinks work as everywhere.
#IRC_SERVER=irc.libera.chat
#IRC_PORT=6697
# TLS is on unless you set exactly "off"; any other value is a configuration
# error rather than a silent downgrade to plaintext.
#IRC_TLS=on
#IRC_NICK=blybot
# Channels to join on connect, comma-separated.
#IRC_CHANNELS=#wikipedia-fr, #wikimedia-tech
# NickServ/server password, if the network needs one. Secret: 0600 file only.
#IRC_PASSWORD=
# PLATFORM=unified runs every platform configured above in ONE process, and
# is what cross-platform bridging needs: a relay has to reach a transport
# the receiving process owns, which separate jobs cannot do. The trade-off
# is deliberate — it gives up the per-platform isolation below, so one
# platform's crash takes them all down. Only set it if you want bridging.
#PLATFORM=unified
#
# Otherwise PLATFORM only matters for a direct single-platform run (python -m blybot);
# the Toolforge deploy overrides it per job, one per platform configured
# above. Leave unset unless you run the app by hand for a single platform.
#PLATFORM=telegram
WIKI_USERNAME=
WIKI_BOTPASSWORD=
# Talk page for /log entries (one section per entry)
LOG_TARGET_PAGE=
# Talk page holding DM discussions (one section per session)
DM_TARGET_BASE=
# WMF User-Agent policy: tool name, version, contact URL/email.
# Example: Blybot/0.1 (https://meta.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Blybot; ops@example.org)
USER_AGENT=

# --- optional (defaults shown) ---------------------------------------------
#BOT_NAME=Blybot
#WIKI_API_URL=https://meta.wikimedia.org/w/api.php
#ALLOWED_GROUP_IDS=
#SESSION_TTL_MINUTES=45
#BURST_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS=8
#TIMESTAMP_GRANULARITY=date
# prompt (in-group deep-link welcome on joins) or off
#NEWCOMER_WELCOME=prompt
# immediate or author_only ("confirm" is reserved for the N1 flow, not yet implemented)
#CONSENT_MODE=immediate
#LOG_THROTTLE_PER_MINUTE=6
#BUG_THROTTLE_PER_HOUR=3
#WIKI_MAX_RETRIES=5
# Seconds before the /log command / the bot's replies are deleted (0 = keep)
#LOG_CLEANUP_SECONDS=5
#REPLY_CLEANUP_SECONDS=15
# --- self-service (v2): enables per-group configuration from Telegram ----
# Leaf appended to every /setpage base path (e.g. "Telegram logs" makes
# "/setpage WikiProject Foo" publish to "WikiProject Foo/Telegram logs").
# Empty disables self-service page targeting.
#WIKI_PAGE_SUFFIX=Telegram logs
# Fernet key enabling ToolsDB profiles + encrypted group tokens. Generate:
#   python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
#PROFILE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
#TOOLSDB_HOST=tools.db.svc.wikimedia.cloud
#TOOLSDB_NAME=
#TOOLSDB_CNF=~/replica.my.cnf
# Minutes between repository-event polls that feed the /rule notifications
#EVENTS_POLL_MINUTES=5

# --- channel intelligence (v3): capture + LLM analyses --------------------
# HMAC key for stable per-scope author pseudonyms; setting it (with
# PROFILE_ENCRYPTION_KEY) enables /capture and the message archive.
# IMPORTANT: capture requires privacy mode OFF at BotFather — read
# docs/OPERATIONS.md before enabling. Generate a key:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
#ARCHIVE_PSEUDONYM_KEY=
# Per-scope ceiling on archived messages per minute (flood guard)
#CAPTURE_MAX_PER_MINUTE=60
# Re-post the capture announcement every N days (0 = only announce once)
#CAPTURE_REANNOUNCE_DAYS=0
# Purge archived messages older than N days on the tick (0 = keep forever)
#CAPTURE_RETENTION_DAYS=0
# Opt-in digest subscriptions ride along automatically on capture-enabled
# deployments (no extra variable). Admins mark a scope subscribable with
# /subscribable on; users then /subscribe in a DM to receive private digests.
# NOTE: this is the one feature that durably stores a subscriber's private
# chat id — see the R6 carve-out in docs/SPECIFICATION.md before enabling.
# LiftWing LLM endpoint and the model ids behind the default/large aliases
#LIFTWING_API_BASE=https://api.wikimedia.org/service/lw/inference/v1
#LIFTWING_MODEL_DEFAULT=llm-qwen3-14b
#LIFTWING_MODEL_LARGE=llm-qwen36-27b
#LIFTWING_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120
# Analyses: default output language, completion-size ceiling, chunk cap
#LLM_DEFAULT_LANG=en
#LLM_MAX_TOKENS_CEILING=4096
#LLM_MAX_CHUNKS_PER_RUN=12
# Total tokens one analysis may spend across all its calls (0 = unlimited)
#LLM_MAX_TOKENS_PER_RUN=200000

# Fine-grained PAT (Issues read/write on the repo only) enabling /bug reports
#GITHUB_TOKEN=
#GITHUB_REPO=schiste/blybot
# Shown in /help, e.g. "User:Schiste (meta.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Schiste)"
#MAINTAINER=
#GROUP_GREETING_TEXT=
#WELCOME_TEXT=
